<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8"/>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"/>
  <meta name="description" content="Terms for the Curb Cut messaging service: what it does, message rates, frequency, support, and how to get help or stop messages."/>
  <title>Terms | Curb Cut</title>
</head>
<body>
<c:CurbCutShell pageTitle="Terms" current="terms"
  eyebrow="Terms and Conditions, updated 30 August 2026"
  heading="You text first. Nothing leaves without your yes."
  lede="That is the whole agreement. The rest is detail the phone companies require us to spell out.">

  <section>
    <div class="plain">
      <span class="tag">The short version</span>
      <p>
        You start the conversation, always. We never message anyone who has not
        messaged us first, and we never send advertising.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing goes to your employer unless you clearly say yes. Your phone
        company may charge you for texts at your normal rate; we charge nothing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Reply <strong>HELP</strong> for help. Reply <strong>STOP</strong> to stop
        the messages.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The programme</h2>
    <table class="facts">
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Name</th><td>Curb Cut</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Number</th>
            <td><span class="kw">+1 276 495 9311</span> &mdash; the number these
                terms are about. If a message reaches you from anywhere else, it
                is not us.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Made by</th>
            <td>Parth Sevak, in Welland, Ontario, Canada</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">What it does</th>
            <td>Helps you work out what could make work easier, and helps you ask
                for it. It can write a request and send it to your employer, but
                only after you have agreed to it in the conversation.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">How you start</th>
            <td>By texting the number yourself. It is printed on a poster at
                work. We never message anyone who has not messaged first. There is
                no mailing list, no upload, and no bought data.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Message frequency</th>
            <td>It varies. Every message is a reply inside a conversation you
                started. Nothing is scheduled and nothing repeats on its own.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Cost</th>
            <td><strong>Message and data rates may apply.</strong> Curb Cut is
                free. Your phone company may still charge you at your usual
                rate.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Help</th>
            <td>Reply <span class="kw">HELP</span>, or email
                <a href="mailto:parth.sevak2@gmail.com">parth.sevak2@gmail.com</a></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Phone companies</th>
            <td>Mobile carriers are not liable for messages that arrive late or
                not at all.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Getting help, and stopping messages</h2>
    <div class="note">
      <p>Reply <strong>HELP</strong> at any time for help and how to reach a person.</p>
      <p>
        Reply <strong>STOP</strong> at any time to stop receiving messages from
        this number. You get one confirmation, then nothing. You can come back
        later by texting again.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      Three other words do something narrower inside a conversation. They are not
      ways to stop messages:
    </p>
    <ul class="kwlist">
      <li><span class="kw">DELETE</span><span>Throw away a drafted request before it is sent.</span></li>
      <li><span class="kw">OFF</span><span>Turn off a preference you saved earlier. Immediate, and you will not be asked why.</span></li>
      <li><span class="kw">WHO</span><span>See everyone who has been shown a preference of yours.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What we promise</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Nothing reaches your employer without your clear yes.</strong>
          A maybe is not a yes. If you say &ldquo;I guess so&rdquo;, you will be
          asked again rather than have it sent.</li>
      <li><strong>We never ask what condition you have</strong>, and there is
          nowhere in the system to keep one.</li>
      <li><strong>We never send you to a phone call.</strong> If you want a
          person, you get one by message.</li>
      <li><strong>We only describe things we have a source for.</strong> If we do
          not know, we say we do not know instead of guessing.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What this is not</h2>
    <p>
      Curb Cut is not a lawyer, a doctor, or your employer. It does not give legal
      or medical advice. It cannot promise any request will be granted, and it does
      not replace your employer's own process or advice from a professional. Cost
      figures come from published Job Accommodation Network survey data and
      describe employers in general, not yours.
    </p>
    <div class="note absent">
      <p>
        <strong>This is not an emergency service.</strong> Nobody is watching it
        around the clock. In an emergency, contact your local emergency services.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Fair use, and the legal bit</h2>
    <p>
      Please use it for what it is for. Do not try to get information about other
      people through it, and do not use it to harass anyone. We may stop serving
      an account used that way.
    </p>
    <p>
      The service is provided as is. So far as the law allows, we are not liable
      for indirect or consequential loss arising from using it. Nothing here takes
      away a right you have that cannot be taken away. These terms are governed by
      the law of Ontario, Canada.
    </p>
    <p>
      How your information is handled is set out in the
      <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>, which is part of these terms.
    </p>
  </section>

</c:CurbCutShell>
</body>
